10:33:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right that a prosthetic limb itself does not develop muscle fatigue: it has no muscles needing oxygen or accumulating metabolic fatigue. A powered device can keep producing assistance as long as its battery and mechanics allow. But the person can still become fatigued—and often may work *harder* than someone using an intact limb—because their remaining body has to compensate. For example: - A passive prosthetic leg does not push off like calf muscles do, so the hips, sound leg, and trunk may have to generate extra force. - A prosthetic arm may require extra shoulder, torso, and visual effort to position and control it. - Socket discomfort, balance corrections, altered gait, and the mental effort of controlling the device can add fatigue. The point of an actively powered “bionic” appendage is partly to replace some of that missing muscle work. If it provides useful push-off or knee assistance, it can reduce the workload on the user’s remaining muscles and therefore reduce *the person’s* fatigue. So: the artificial limb does not tire, but whether its user is less fatigued depends on whether the device actually offsets enough of the missing biological function to reduce whole-body effort.
