10:33:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It supports the narrower point that **a prosthetic limb does not experience local muscle fatigue**, and that can change where fatigue occurs during running. Carbon-fiber sprinting blades are passive springs: they store energy when loaded and return part of it, but they do not create net energy like a motorized e-bike. Because there are no calf, ankle, or foot muscles in the blade, the athlete does not fatigue those missing muscles. Some athletes may therefore report less lower-leg muscular fatigue, or a different fatigue pattern, than an able-bodied runner. But that does not automatically mean they have **less total physical fatigue overall** than regular runners: - They still must supply running power with their hips, thighs, core, and—in unilateral amputees—the intact leg. - Prosthetic running can involve different balance, impact, socket-pressure, and control demands. - The blades return only some of the energy put into them; they are not a free energy source. - Elite Paralympic sprinters and elite non-amputee sprinters are difficult to compare directly because their biomechanics, training, bodies, and race classifications differ. So the article may well be correct about reduced fatigue in particular muscles or a different sensation of fatigue. It does not establish that a blade user necessarily expends less whole-body energy or is universally less tired than a comparable non-amputee runner. Your broader intuition remains sound: if the appendage is **actively powered** and supplies meaningful work, it can reduce whole-body effort—more directly like e-bike assistance. Carbon blades mainly redistribute and recycle mechanical energy; powered bionic limbs can potentially add energy.
