10:36:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target: If you mean **“there is no fatigue in the replaced limb’s muscles,”** then yes—by definition, that is less *local limb-muscle fatigue* for the person, because those muscles are no longer present. But people normally use “I’m fatigued” to mean **whole-person exhaustion**: how tired their remaining muscles feel, how hard their heart and lungs are working, pain or socket discomfort, and whether they can keep doing the activity. That is not simply the sum of whether each body part contains muscle. For example, a person can have no fatigue at all in a missing calf, yet be more exhausted overall because their other leg, hips, and back are doing extra work. Conversely, a powered prosthesis can take over enough work that they are less exhausted overall. So there is no dispute about the basic fact: > The artificial leg does not get muscle-fatigued; a sufficiently helpful powered leg can make its user less fatigued. The only point is that the first fact alone does not prove the second in every situation.
